WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.575
Unit 2 is Attack Surface Reduction.

2
00:00:02.575 --> 00:00:06.788
Microsoft Learn's rule modes
are zero for off, one for block,

3
00:00:06.788 --> 00:00:10.650
two for audit, five for not
configured, and six for warn.

4
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.478
Before you audit, Learn
says to turn off any

5
00:00:13.478 --> 00:00:16.387
related rule that's
already in block or warn.

6
00:00:16.387 --> 00:00:19.340
Then put every rule in
audit at once on ring one,

7
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.924
which Learn recommends, to see which
rules everyday business triggers.

8
00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:27.175
The program's stages are
risk-based recommendations.

9
00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:29.617
Audit on ring one for thirty days,

10
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:34.412
until every audited rule hit has
an exclusion, a fix or a decision.

11
00:00:34.412 --> 00:00:38.790
Then warn, first on ring one and
then on ring two, for fourteen days,

12
00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.589
counting unblock requests,
until no business-critical

13
00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:45.975
application is left without
a per-rule exclusion.

14
00:00:45.975 --> 00:00:49.572
Then block, ring by ring,
until blocks per rule per week

15
00:00:49.572 --> 00:00:53.250
are stable and help-desk
volume is under the threshold.

16
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.202
Microsoft's own position,
from its rules overview,

17
00:00:56.202 --> 00:01:01.027
is that the standard protection rules can
go to block without extensive testing,

18
00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.475
and other rules are tested in audit first.

19
00:01:03.475 --> 00:01:07.560
The program runs all three
stages anyway, and says so.
